## Highlights
- There’s a concept in evolutionary biology called the ‘evolutionary mismatch.’ In essence, it’s an adaptive lag that occurs when an organism’s environment changes *faster* than it can adapt. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j0sc29rgn4xfqwt0q198vs8f))
- All of this would be bad enough if the issue were only the *quantity* of the information and our Stone Age brains’ tendency to get easily distracted by novelty. Unfortunately, it’s not.
  Even kids today have a name for it: ‘brain rot.’ ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j1gq06ewmcn6mbt6f5eazv6r))
- Generative AI is also a massive contributor to the rising pollution of our information environments. Some estimates suggest that AI already produces up to 10% of all online content and that by 2026, this could [increase to a staggering 90%.](https://futurism.com/the-byte/experts-90-online-content-ai-generated) How much of that will be of decent quality? How much of it will be insightful and helpful? ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j1gq2xe0qzd8h5b4b7h5btyv))
- Still, just as we’ve started to clean the air, water, and soil, it’s time we found ways to clean up the pollution that directly affects our minds, too. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j1keg0q5kam8aer2mhtbq9g4))


